{"format":1,"seq":174,"writes":[{"type":"entry","value":{"model":[{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Since PitContextState is a shared, generated contract, I shouldn't add a new \"collapsed\" field to it. Instead, I can reuse the existing \"elided\" state to represent collapsed outline entries, keeping things contract-compatible without introducing new fields.\n\nNow I'm planning the actual implementation: extending ContextOperation with \"collapse\", mapping both \"elide\" and \"collapse\" operations to the \"elided\" state, refactoring context-view reading into helper functions, and defining a COLLAPSED_PREFIX constant so previews can show the collapsed note.\n\nI'm figuring out where run segmentation logic belongs — putting the collapse planning and line-building in a new collapse.ts file alongside edits.ts, with a helper for the replacement note that strips tool-call blocks while keeping thinking/text content. I'm also working out how to group conversation items into \"runs\" based on where user/custom messages break the sequence, handling the edge case of items before the first user input belonging to a leading run tied to any compaction summary.\n\nFor keeping runs, I need to retain the newest keepRuns-1 among older runs and treat the rest as collapse candidates. I'm defining the exact eligibility criteria for an assistant item to be collapsible: it must be an unmodified, unprotected, non-pending assistant message with tool calls, whose results are also all original and unprotected, and whose replacement token count must come in lower than the original.\n\nFor the minTokens threshold, I'm weighing whether to compare against total view tokens or just the conversation tokens excluding system/tools overhead, since the issue frames it as \"active context.\" I'm leaning toward using the full view tokens to match that framing, though I'm noting that for pi-action's fixed 41K context this threshold will essentially always trigger collapsing, which seems acceptable since collapsing small threads isn't harmful.\n\nI'm settling on measuring transcript tokens (messages only, excluding system) with a 32K threshold, reasoning that below that point the fixed prompt dominates cost savings while above it the transcript itself becomes the bigger factor — verified against the thread #383 example to confirm the logic holds. Now I'm moving into designing the apply.ts task input structure.\n\nFor the commit phase, I need to read staged entries and, if collapsing, determine the active range, build a view that protects the current run via the \"first\" parameter, and plan the collapse. I'm also considering an edge case: staged edits from a previous failed run that never got applied would remain stuck in the pending map indefinitely, since collectEdits treats unlisted pending entries as still pending forever.\n\nThis is a pre-existing quirk I'll accept conservatively — those targets just skip collapse. I'm assembling the final edit set from staged edits plus collapse edits, building the operations list with collapse provenance, and handling conflicts (collapse skips any pending targets, including ones covered by a summarize range, and model edits applied during the current run on older entries get skipped since their state no longer matches the original). I'm also sketching the signature for createApplyEditsHook taking an optional CollapseSettings and returning a task.\n\nI'm deciding to gate the task to run only when there are pending edits or a collapse is configured, accepting the extra per-run cost, and I'll need to replace the APPLY_STAGED_EDITS_HOOK export with this factory and update the planning test that references it directly. I'm also confirming idempotence holds across crash-and-rerun, that terminate-reason runs follow the same path with run.last as the newest protected result, and that compaction views start at head with pre-head entries excluded. Finally I'm working through how thinking content is preserved in replacements — filtering out toolCall blocks and appending a text note to the original content.\n\nNow I'm worried about whether consecutive assistant turns containing thinking blocks will get merged by the Anthropic API, since docs say consecutive same-role turns combine into one turn, and thinking blocks might need to sit at the start of that merged turn or get stripped from non-final turns. I need to verify this directly against the real API behavior, and check whether pi-ai's provider does its own message merging, before deciding whether collapsed notes need a different shape.\n\nI'll just run a real probe: first get an actual thinking signature via a tool-use call, then assemble the collapsed context and test it. Since there's no tsx/jiti/vite-node runner available, I'll write a plain .mjs script importing the pi-ai package's JS dist directly and check its exports for `complete`/`stream` and provider setup.\n\n","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_bdrk_01EZY8SiNGVFXiAEqyqoGzbB","name":"typescript","arguments":{"code":"// @pit: {\"label\": \"Inspect pi-ai exports for a direct anthropic-messages probe\"}\nasync ({ shell: { exec } }) => {\n  const r = await exec(`cd node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-ai && grep '\"version\"' package.json && node -e 'const p=require(\"./package.json\");console.log(Object.keys(p.exports).join(\" \"))' && grep -n \"export\" dist/index.d.ts | head -40 && ls dist/providers | head -40`);\n  return r.stdout + r.stderr;\n}"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"nvidia","model":"aws/anthropic/bedrock-claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":3263,"cacheRead":92139,"cacheWrite":717,"totalTokens":96121,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0,"totalFromProvider":false},"cacheWrite1h":0,"reasoning":3034},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1794904584079,"responseId":"msg_bdrk_f3b4u5bu3yuk2gmgqjg43yfl723e2cqjs7uymwlqngfv5t7bvq3q","rawStopReason":"tool_use","durationMs":36918,"thinkingLevel":"medium"}],"kind":"pi.assistant","id":55,"conversationId":1,"byTaskId":54}},{"type":"task","value":{"id":54,"conversationId":1,"kind":"pi.generation","version":2,"input":{},"background":false,"abortRequested":false,"state":{"status":"waiting","checkpoint":{"phase":"tools","assistant":55,"tools":[56],"pending":[]},"on":[56],"policy":"allSettled"},"startedAt":1794904584045}},{"type":"task","value":{"id":56,"conversationId":1,"kind":"pi.tool","version":3,"input":{"kind":"model","assistant":55,"callId":"toolu_bdrk_01EZY8SiNGVFXiAEqyqoGzbB"},"owner":54,"background":false,"abortRequested":false,"state":{"status":"pending","checkpoint":{"phase":"call"}}}},{"type":"document.change","id":2,"content":{"version":3,"kind":"base","value":{"run":{"taskId":54,"inputs":[8],"turns":12,"first":7},"tools":[{"callId":"toolu_bdrk_01EZY8SiNGVFXiAEqyqoGzbB","name":"typescript","taskId":56,"status":"pending"}]}}},{"type":"document.change","id":4,"content":{"version":1,"kind":"base","value":{"models":{"nvidia/aws/anthropic/bedrock-claude-opus-5-5":{"input":26,"output":14755,"cacheRead":651738,"cacheWrite":92856,"totalTokens":759375,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0,"totalFromProvider":false},"cacheWrite1h":0,"reasoning":11620}},"tools":{}}}}]}